00429534912109375, -0.02740478515625, -0.022308349609375, 0.0269927978515625, -0.032562255859375, 0.00847625732421875, 0.045501708984375, 0.03253173828125, -0.0122222900390625, 0.00616455078125, 0.017181396484375, 0.0179901123046875, 0.024444580078125, -0.0282440185546875, -0.02276611328125, 0.026885986328125, 0.0153045654296875, 0.005828857421875, -0.01336669921875, 0.006275177001953125, -0.032684326171875, 0.00107574462890625, 0.03948974609375, -0.01293182373046875, -2.092123031616211e-05, 0.02984619140625, -0.0210418701171875, 0.017059326171875, -0.0220794677734375, 0.01308441162109375, -0.00887298583984375, 0.0076141357421875, 0.0234375, 0.0196380615234375, -0.007049560546875, 0.01042938232421875, 0.0138702392578125, -0.01456451416015625, 0.01316070556640625, -0.01502227783203125, 0.0253143310546875, 0.04132080078125, -0.046600341796875, 0.01392364501953125, -0.0029048919677734375, 0.0193023681640625, 0.0179443359375, -0.025146484375, 0.0117340087890625, 0.0095367431640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and complex meshed (cyclic) topologies. In such cyclic networks with multiple suppliers and storage units, operational optimization is complicated by nonlinear hydraulic and thermal interactions, inter-temporal coupling via storage, and the combinatorial complexity of flow directions in cycles. Existing methods typically address only acyclic or linearized models, or cannot efficiently guarantee global optimality for realistic, multi-period, cyclic networks with nonlinearities and storage. For example, in a network with two suppliers and a storage tank connected in a loop, determining the optimal flow and temperature profiles over time requires resolving nonlinear pressure drops (which relate flow to pressure loss in pipes), mixing of water at network junctions (which determines outgoing temperatures), and storage dynamics (which couple decisions across time), all while avoiding non-physical cyclic flows\u2014flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand. Standard linear or tree-based approaches do not address these challenges. To our knowledge, no prior work provides a global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, along with scalable algorithmic enhancements and comprehensive evaluation on realistic benchmarks. This work fills that gap by presenting a unified modeling, algorithmic, and evaluation framework, with explicit discussion of assumptions and limitations.\n\n---\n\n**Physical Model and Key Assumptions**  \nWe model the district heating network as a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\), where nodes \\( V \\) represent junctions, suppliers, consumers, or storage units, and edges \\( E \\) represent pipes. The following physical assumptions are made for tractability and clarity:\n\n- **Perfect Mixing:** Water at each node is assumed to mix instantaneously and perfectly, so outgoing temperature is a flow-weighted average of incoming streams. This is a standard simplification (see [1]) and is reasonable for well-designed junctions with turbulent flow.\n- **Neglect of Heat Losses:** Heat losses along pipes are neglected; all pipes are assumed perfectly insulated. This is justified for short time horizons, well-insulated networks, or when heat losses are small relative to total flows (e.g., <5% per hour, as in [2]). For networks with significant heat losses or over long horizons, this assumption may lead to optimistic efficiency estimates; see Limitations below for discussion and possible model extensions.\n- **Constant Fluid Properties:** Water density and specific heat are assumed constant and independent of temperature and pressure, which is standard for moderate temperature ranges (e.g., 50\u2013120\u00b0C) in district heating.\n- **No Minimum Flow Thresholds:** Minimum flow rates in pipes are neglected, assuming that operational flows always exceed practical thresholds (e.g., >0.1 kg/s). If flows approach zero, additional constraints may be needed to ensure physical feasibility.\n- **No Pipe Capacity Limits:** Unless otherwise stated, pipe flow rates are assumed unbounded for modeling simplicity; practical limits can be added as linear constraints. This is reasonable for preliminary design or when pipe sizing is not a limiting factor, but may overestimate feasible flows in highly constrained networks.\n- **No Pressure-Driven Demand:** Consumer demands are assumed fixed and independent of network pressure, which is appropriate when consumer-side controls maintain demand regardless of supply pressure.\n\nThese assumptions are standard in the literature for first-principles modeling and allow focus on the core nonlinear and combinatorial challenges. Their impact on solution validity is discussed in the Limitations section below; in scenarios where these assumptions do not hold, model extensions are possible.\n\n---\n\n**Limitations and Applicability**  \nThe above assumptions enable tractable modeling and optimization but introduce limitations:\n- **Heat Loss Neglect:** In networks with long pipe runs or poor insulation, neglecting heat losses can overestimate efficiency and underestimate supply requirements. For such cases, including heat loss terms (e.g., exponential decay along pipes) is recommended.\n- **No Pipe Capacity Limits:** Omitting pipe flow limits may yield solutions that are infeasible in practice if actual pipe diameters are small. For operational planning, pipe-specific maximum flows should be enforced.\n- **No Minimum Flows:** If actual operation involves very low or intermittent flows, the model may suggest unphysical solutions; adding minimum flow constraints addresses this.\n- **Fixed Demands:** The model does not capture demand-side flexibility or pressure-dependent demand, which may be relevant in some advanced control scenarios.\n- **Perfect Mixing:** In rare cases with poor mixing or stratification, the temperature mixing assumption may not hold.\n- **Short Time Horizons:** The model is most accurate for short to moderate time horizons (e.g., hours to a day); for long-term planning, additional dynamics (e.g., heat losses, maintenance) may need to be included.\n\nUsers should assess the relevance of these limitations for their specific application. Further discussion and possible model extensions are provided in the supplementary material.\n\n---\n\n**Main Contributions and Structure**  \nTo address the above challenges, this work makes the following contributions, with each section building on the previous:\n\n- **Global Optimization Framework:** We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints under the above physical assumptions.\n- **Explicit Problem Formulation:** We provide a summary of the full nonlinear mixed-integer optimization problem, including variables, objective, and main constraints, with intuitive explanations of their physical meaning.\n- **Algorithmic Enhancements:** We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks. For example, (1) cycle-breaking constraints are introduced to eliminate non-physical cyclic flows by enforcing that the sum of flows around any fundamental cycle is zero unless serving demand; mathematically, for a cycle \\( C \\), \\( \\sum_{e \\in C} q_{e,t} = 0 \\) if no net demand is present. (2) Symmetry-breaking constraints reduce redundant search by fixing flow directions in a canonical order, e.g., requiring \\( x_{e_1,t} \\geq x_{e_2,t} \\) for edges \\( e_1, e_2 \\) in a cycle. The remaining enhancements\u2014strengthened relaxations for nonlinear pressure drops, valid inequalities for storage operation, and adaptive branching strategies\u2014are detailed in the supplementary material, with key steps summarized below.\n- **Comprehensive Evaluation:** We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances. Benchmarks include synthetic networks (5\u201330 nodes, 1\u20135 cycles) and real-world topologies from two European cities. Parameter ranges cover supplier capacities (1\u201320 MW), storage sizes (10\u2013200 MWh), and demand profiles (hourly, with 10\u201330% variability). Main evaluation metrics are solution time, optimality gap, and computational scalability. Key findings: our enhancements reduce solution time by up to 60% on large cyclic instances, and global optimality is achieved for all tested cases within 2% optimality gap.\n- **Time Decomposition Analysis:** We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, rigorously discussing its assumptions, empirical validity, and limitations.\n- **Discussion and Future Work:** We discuss limitations in detail, provide concrete next steps, and outline plans for open-sourcing code and data, including current availability.\n\nEach section is introduced and concluded with brief summaries to guide the reader through the modeling, algorithmic, and evaluation components.\n\n---\n\n**Notation and Symbol Table**  \nFor clarity, we define the main symbols used throughout the formulation. We use node indices (\\( v \\)) for variables associated with nodes (e.g., \\( T_{v,t} \\)), and edge indices (\\( e \\)) for variables associated with pipes (e.g., \\( q_{e,t} \\)). Temperatures at pipe inlets are denoted \\( T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}} \\), which equals the outgoing temperature of the upstream node: \\( T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}} = T_{u(e),t}^{\\text{out}} \\), where \\( u(e) \\) is the upstream node of edge \\( e \\).\n\n| Symbol | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| \\( q_{e,t} \\) | Flow rate (mass per unit time) in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (positive/negative indicates direction) |\n| \\( T_{v,t} \\) | Water temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}} \\) | Temperature of water entering pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (equals \\( T_{u(e),t}^{\\text{out}} \\)) |\n| \\( T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} \\) | Outgoing temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( s_{j,t} \\) | State of charge (energy stored) for storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( x_{e,t} \\) | Binary variable indicating flow direction in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (1 if forward, 0 if reverse) |\n| \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\) | Non-negative variables for energy charged/discharged from storage \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( \\Delta p_{e,t} \\) | Pressure drop across pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( k_e \\) | Pipe friction coefficient for pipe \\( e \\) |\n| \\( \\eta_{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} \\) | Charging/discharging efficiency for storage units |\n| \\( T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\), \\( T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\) | Min/max allowable temperatures for supplier \\( i \\) |"}}